- Decide what the player clicked

- When the user clicks a square, convert that click into bitboard mask for that square

- Then check whether that square contains a piece.

- If it does not, ignore the click.

- Pick the first square and the second square

- Store the first clicked square as the “from” square.

- On the next click, treat that as the “to” square.

- If the player clicks the same piece again, just deselect it.

Based on the piece at the “from” square, decide whether the “to” square is a legal move.

pawn: one step forward, two-step from start, captures diagonally

knight: L-shape

bishop/rook/queen: sliding moves

king: one square in any direction

A good structure is:

read the piece at the from-square
determine legal targets
if the target is legal, update the board
otherwise, do nothing
